  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}\u2061\\theta ={sec}^{2}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x)(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)} & =\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}\u2061x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x={sin}^{2}\u2061x,\\,\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x},\\,\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}\u2061x)} & =\\frac{1}{({sin}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x)(1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x)}={cot}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
